1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louis François I de Bourbon, Prince de Conti (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #113748, b. 13 August 1717, d. 2 August 1776
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=4 Mar 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT      Louis François I de Bourbon, Prince de Conti was born on 13 August 1717 at Paris, France. (2) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Louis Armand II de Bourbon, Prince de Conti and Louise Elizabeth de Condé. (2) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Louise Diane d'Orléans, daughter of Philippe II d'Orléans, Duc d'Orléans and Françoise Marie de Bourbon, on 22 January 1732 at Versailles, Île-de-France, France. (2) He died on 2 August 1776 at age 58 at Paris, France. 
2 CONC (2)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Louis François I de Bourbon, Prince de Conti gained the title of Prince de Conti.1 He had another son.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Louis François I de Bourbon, Prince de Conti and  Louise Diane d'Orléans
2 CONT
2 CONT -1.Louis François de Conti, Prince de Conti+ b. 1 Sep 1734, d. 13 Mar 1814
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p11375.htm#i113748
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Fran%C3%A7ois_I,_Prince_of_Conti
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis François de Bourbon, Prince of Conti (13 August 1717 – 2 August 1776) was a French nobleman, who was the Prince of Conti from 1727 to his death, following his father Louis Armand II. His mother was Louise Élisabeth de Bourbo
2 CONC n, a natural granddaughter of Louis XIV. As a member of the reigning House of Bourbon, he was a Prince du Sang.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis François I de Bourbon was born in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1731, he married his cousin, Louise Diane d'Orléans, Mademoiselle de Chartres, the youngest daughter of Philippe d'Orléans, the Régent of France during the minority of King Louis XV of France, and his wife Françoise-Marie de Bo
2 CONC urbon, Mademoiselle de Blois (daughter of Madame de Montespan).
2 CONT
2 CONT His marriage was organised by his mother the Dowager Princesss of Conti and future mother in law the Dowager Duchess of Orléans.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in childbirth at the Château d'Issy in 1736. His younger sister, Louise Henriette de Bourbon was the mother of Philippe Égalité. He then stayed at L'Isle-Adam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis François pursued a military career, and when the War of the Austrian Succession broke out in 1741, he accompanied the Duke of Belle-Isle to Bohemia. His services there led to his command of the army in Italy, where he distin
2 CONC guished himself by forcing the pass of Villafranca and winning the battle of Coni in 1744. http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Villafranca_(1744) http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Casteldelfino
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1745 he was sent to check the Austrians in Germany. In 1746, he was transferred to the Netherlands, where conflicts with the Maréchal de Saxe led to his retirement in 1747 to the Château de L'Isle-Adam.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1760 he bought a famous Burgundy vineyard which at that time bore the name of La Romanée, at a high price. After the purchase he added his own name to the vineyard, which since has been known as Romanée-Conti. The wine from thi
2 CONC s vineyard is one of the world's most expensive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Candidate to the Polish Throne
2 CONT
2 CONT In that same year, a faction of Polish nobles offered Conti the throne of Poland, where King Augustus III was expected to die soon. Conti was able to win the personal support of Louis XV of France for his candidacy. However, the p
2 CONC olicy of the king's ministers was to establish the ruling house of Saxony upon the throne in Poland, as Louis XV's daughter-in-law, Marie-Josèphe of Saxony, was a daughter of the ailing Augustus. As a result of this conflict, Loui
2 CONC s XV began secret communications with his ambassadors at certain influential foreign courts that were in direct opposition to the official communications being sent to those same ambassadors by his ministers. The system of courier
2 CONC s used to relay the king's secret messages developed later into a spy-network known as the Secret du Roi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Conti did not secure the Polish throne, he did remain in the confidence of the king until 1755, when his influence was destroyed by the intrigues of the king's mistress, Madame de Pompadour. His relationship with Louis XV
2 CONC  deteriorated so, that when the Seven Years' War broke out in 1756, Conti was refused the command of the army of the Rhine. Angry, he began opposing the royal government, which caused Louis to refer to him as, "my cousin, the advo
2 CONC cate".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1771, Conti took the lead in opposing the chancellor, Maupeou. He supported the parlements against the government and was especially hostile to Turgot. Due to the intensity of his anti-government feelings, he was suspected of a
2 CONC iding an uprising which took place in Dijon in 1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conti inherited literary tastes from his father, was a brave and skillful general, and a diligent student of military history. His mistress, the cultivated Comtesse de Boufflers (1725–1800), presided over a salon at his home in Pa
2 CONC ris, which attracted many men of letters. Through his mistress, he became a patron of Jean Jacques Rousseau.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his son, Louis François Joseph (1734–1814), who was the last person to bear the Conti title.
