1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ref: https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Madison/02-09-02-0404 From a letter To James Madison from William Neilson, 15 May 1805, of the Marine Insurance Office, dated May 15, 1805.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Blockade of Curacao "Abraham S. Hallett’s brig Bulah Maria, Capt. Hezekiah Pinkham of Hallett’s schooner Favourite, Capt. Lemuel Bourne of Snell, Stagg, & Co.’s brig Lapwing, Capt. Isaiah Smart of Jacques Ruden’s schooner Perf
2 CONC ormance, and Capt. Thomas Brown of the schooner Bethia owned by James Parker, Joseph Marsh, and Cortlandt L. Parker of Perth Amboy, New Jersey. In many cases the mates and supercargoes of the ships deposed also. All the vessels bu
2 CONC t the Bethia sailed from New York; all were bound for Curaçao laden with foodstuffs, wines, dry goods, tobacco, or lumber. All were owned by American citizens and sailed between October and December 1804. All were seized off Bonai
2 CONC re during November and December 1804, and January 1805; three by the Franchise, Capt. John Murray, and two by the Diana, Capt. Thomas James Maling, and the Pelican, Capt. John Marshall. They were taken into Kingston and Port Royal
2 CONC , Jamaica, and condemned by the vice-admiralty court at Kingston".
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
