1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''please don't merge with''' [https://www.geni.com/people/William-Stewart/6000000179991587826] - '''it's not proven that Lt. Gen. Sir William Stewart was married to Mary Anne. Lt. Gen. Sir William Stewart was marr
2 CONC ied to Frances Douglas, who died 1833, no divorce are fixed probably, so Lt. Gen. Sir William Stewart can't be legitimate father of children born prior 1833. In birth records of chjildren (Horatio, Mary Anne, etc) there are no sig
2 CONC ns, that their father was the Lt. Gen. Sir William Stewart  Please write a reliable source about marriage Lt. Gen. Sir William Stewart and Mary Anne or records of children to prove that Williams are same! (online trees on myherita
2 CONC ge and other "sources" is not reliable for this reason!)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Also '''Sir William leaved in at Cumloden on the borders of Wigton and Kirkcudbrightshire from 1818 and died there 7 Jan 1827 (see''' https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Stewart,_William_(177
2 CONC 4-1827)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''his alleged "son" Horatio Stewart christened at Saint Mary, Battersea, London, England 18 Dec 1825''' (see https://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-30042/%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D1%8F-%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81
2 CONC %D0%B8-%D0%BE-%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B6%D0%B4%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B8-%D0%B8-%D0%BA%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%89%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B8-1538-1975?lang=RU&itemId=29703848-&action=showRecord&recordTitle=Horatio+Stewart)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Stewart_(1774%E2%80%931827)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General Sir William Stewart, GCB (10 January 1774 – 7 January 1827) was a British military officer who was the first Commanding Officer of the Rifle Corps, a Division Commander in the Peninsula and a Scottish Member of 
2 CONC Parliament (MP) in the British Parliament. He was the fourth son of John Stewart, 7th Earl of Galloway.
2 CONT
2 CONT He represented Saltash in Cornwall from 1795 to 1796, Wigtownshire 1796-1802, the Wigtown Burghs 1803-1805 and Wigtownshire again 1812-1816.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early military success
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart entered the British Army in 1786 as a twelve year-old Ensign in the 42nd Foot. His first active service was in the West Indies Campaign of 1793-94, where he was wounded. After further service in the West Indies, when he co
2 CONC mmanded the 67th Foot at San Domingo (1796–98), Stewart returned to Europe and was given permission to serve with Britain's Austrian and Russian allies in Italy, Swabia and Switzerland during the campaign of 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart was intensely interested in weapons and tactics. It was probably his observations in 1799 of 'light infantry' and Tyrolese and Croat soldiers that did not fight in the rigid formations adopted by normal infantry units that
2 CONC  led him to propose that the British Army should include a permanent force of 'light infantry', equipped with rifles. His ideas won support, especially from the influential Equerry to the King, Colonel Coote Manningham, who Stewar
2 CONC t had first met in the West Indies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1800 an experimental 'Corps of Riflemen' was established. In August Stewart commanded it at the amphibious attack on Ferrol, where he was severely wounded in the chest as he led his riflemen up the cliffs. In October 1800
2 CONC  the Corps was gazetted as an established unit, with Manningham as Colonel and Stewart as its first Lieutenant-Colonel and Commanding Officer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart's Standing Orders for the Rifle Corps, which later became the famous 95th Foot (Rifle Brigade), show how advanced his tactical thinking was compared to that of his contemporaries. He devised and implemented specially adapt
2 CONC ed forms of drill and manoeuvre, medals for bravery and good conduct, classification in shooting ability, a school and a library for the soldiers, while requiring every Rifles officer to get to know each of his men as individuals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after Stewart's twenty-seventh birthday he was appointed to command the 895 soldiers (114 from the Rifle Corps and 781 from the 49th Regiment) that were to serve as marines in the fleet sent to the Baltic in 1801. He was s
2 CONC tationed on the quarter-deck of Admiral Nelson's flagship HMS Elephant throughout the great naval battle of Copenhagen on 2 April 1801. Nelson reported that "The Honourable Colonel Stewart did me the favour to be aboard the Elepha
2 CONC nt; and himself, with every officer and soldier under his orders, shared with pleasure the toils and dangers of the day" (Stewart's detachment lost 4 dead and 6 wounded).
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart was chosen for the honour of carrying to London the despatches reporting the victory and was included by name in the Thanks of Parliament voted on April 16, 1801. Six days later he received an official letter of promotion 
2 CONC to full Colonel, effective from the day of the Battle of Copenhagen. Nelson wrote to Lord St Vincent praising "Colonel Stewart, who is an excellent and indefatigable young man, and depend upon it, the rising hope of our army".
2 CONT
2 CONT Nelson wrote at least eleven letters to Stewart in the four years between Copenhagen and his death at Trafalgar, which were included in the collection of Stewart's papers privately published as "The Cumloden Papers".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1802 the Rifle Corps was redesignated as the 95th (Rifle) Regiment and together with the 43rd and 52nd Foot was formed into the famous Light Brigade commanded by Sir John Moore. Stewart was the first Colonel of the 95th, but so
2 CONC on had to hand over its operational command when he was appointed to be a Brigadier-General. Stewart's heart still lay with the Rifles and in 1805 he published "Outlines of a Plan for the General Reform of the British Land Forces"
2 CONC  which advocated general adoption of many of the innovations he had already made within the 95th.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart held important commands in the expeditions to Egypt in 1807 and to Walcheren in 1809, before being sent to Spain in 1810. Although Stewart was still junior as a Major-General, he was given the crucial task of commanding th
2 CONC e besieged garrison of the vital port of Cadiz and initially put directly under the orders of General Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington. This led to an appointment to command a Brigade in the Second Division of the army in 
2 CONC the Peninsula, and in December 1810 Stewart took over as commander of Second Division.
2 CONT
2 CONT Division commander under Wellington
2 CONT
2 CONT At the Battle of Albuera on May 16, 1811, Stewart led the all-British 2nd Division, which bore the brunt of Marshal Nicolas Soult's flank attack. He wheeled Lieut-Col John Colborne's brigade to attack the left flank of Soult's mas
2 CONC sive French column. At first, the manoeuvre went well, as British musketry savaged the French infantry. Suddenly attacked from flank and rear by mounted Polish lancers and French hussars, three of Colborne's regiments were massacr
2 CONC ed, losing 1,250 men; only 400 escaped. At that battle, Stewart's other two brigades also suffered severely from point-blank French cannon and musket fire, but this was not his fault. In an epic struggle, the survivors of his divi
2 CONC sion held back the French until the 4th Division saved the day. Glover, historian of the Peninsular War, wrote, "As a battalion commander, Stewart was surpassed only by Moore; as a general he was a menace."
2 CONT
2 CONT Wellington wrote of him, "It is necessary that Stewart should be under the particular charge of somebody." After Albuera, Wellington found that "somebody" in the person of Lieutenant-General Rowland Hill. For the rest of the Penin
2 CONC sular War, Stewart and his 2nd Division usually served under Hill's competent supervision. He fought in Hill's corps in the Burgos campaign in the fall of 1812 and at the Battle of Vitoria in 1813.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 15, 1812, Soult's 80,000 Frenchmen confronted Wellington's 65,000 Anglo-Portuguese near Salamanca. When Soult failed to attack, Wellington ordered a withdrawal to Portugal. During the retreat, Stewart (temporarily in c
2 CONC harge of the 1st Division) and two other division commanders disobeyed their commander's orders. Stewart, Wellington wrote, "and certain other generals held a Council of War to decide whether to obey my orders to march by a partic
2 CONC ular road. He [Stewart], at the head, decided they would not; they marched by a road leading they knew not where, and when I found them in the morning they were in the utmost confusion, not knowing where to go and what to do."
2 CONT
2 CONT On the opening day of the Battle of the Pyrenees at Maya Pass, Stewart concluded that the French would not attack, then rode ten miles to the rear. When the battle began, his 2nd Division was left to fight all morning under an ine
2 CONC xperienced brigade commander and lost 1,347 men. Still in Hill's corps, Stewart fought at the battles of the Nive, Orthez and Toulouse during Wellington's 1814 invasion of southern France.
