1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Anne_Hamilton_(1766%E2%80%931846)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Hamilton (16 March 1766 – 10 October 1846) was a courtier and writer, and friend of the British queen Caroline of Brunswick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Lady Anne Hamilton was born in 1766. She was the daughter of Archibald Hamilton, 9th Duke of Hamilton.
2 CONT
2 CONT She became a lady-in-waiting to Caroline of Brunswick, Princess of Wales and estranged wife of the Prince Regent, replacing Hester Lisle in 1812.[1] She held this post until the following year, when the Princess went into voluntar
2 CONC y exile in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne had radical sensibilities [2] and, on the Prince, succeeding as George IV on 29 January 1820, repeatedly urged Caroline to return and claim her position as Queen Consort of Great Britain. She and radicals such as Henry B
2 CONC rougham and William Cobbett saw the Queen as a focus for the reformist Whig opposition. She crossed to France to meet Caroline at St. Omer, and with Alderman Matthew Wood, a radical former Lord Mayor of London, escorted her back t
2 CONC o the capital.[3] She resumed her position in Caroline's household, accompanying her to her trial for adultery in the House of Lords in August 1820, and remaining almost her sole supporter among ladies of consequence[4] until her 
2 CONC acquittal. When Caroline sought admittance to the Coronation in Westminster Abbey to take her rightful place beside George on 19 July 1821, Lady Anne Hamilton and Lady Hood were her two ladies-in-waiting.[5] Being debarred and hum
2 CONC iliated broke Caroline's spirit, and Hamilton was with her until her death on 7 August 1821[6] and her burial in Brunswick later that month.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne was described by Creevey, at the trial, as "full six feet high and bears a striking resemblance to one of Lord Derby's great red deer"[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Hamilton published a satirical epic poem called Epics of the Ton in 1807.[9] The work, which was published anonymously, satirized the main figures involved in what was called "The Delicate Investigation" of the morality 
2 CONC and suspected adultery of Caroline of Brunswick.[10] Hamilton referred to the main characters by their initials.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Moments of Pleasure - a satirical print by Theodore Lane incorporating Lady Anne Hamilton; Sir Matthew Wood, 1st Bt and Caroline of Brunswick.
2 CONT In 1805 and in 1815 she was painted by James Lonsdale and these portraits were exhibited at the Royal Academy.[11] Lady Anne was also included in a number of satirical prints by Theodore Lane in 1820 and 1821.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1832 a book was published which claimed to reveal facts about the royal household. It was titled Secret History of the Court of England from the accession of George the Third until the Death of George the Fourth and was attribu
2 CONC ted to "Lady Anne Hamilton".[9] This book was written by a woman who had gained Hamilton's confidence and is referred to as "S.W." in correspondence. A strong suspect for this role is Olivia Serres (born Wilmot). The resulting sca
2 CONC ndal meant that the publisher had to flee to avoid prosecution and Hamilton had to spend some time in France.[13] However, a subsequent biographer of Queen Caroline attributes the record of her demeanor and words as she lay dying 
2 CONC directly to Hamilton as author of this work. [14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamilton died in White Lion Street, Islington, in 1846.[15] She was buried in Kensal Green Cemetery.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/KC32-ZF1/anne-hamilton-1766-1846
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Hamilton-11502
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/women/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/hamilton-anne-1766-1846
2 CONT https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Hamilton,_Anne_(1766-1846)
2 CONT https://www.allabouthistory.co.uk/History/Scotland/Person/Anne-Hamilton-1766-1846.html
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/124110299/anne-hamilton
2 CONT
2 CONT She was an Scottish courtier and memoirist. She was the eldest daughter of Archibald Hamilton, ninth duke of Hamilton, and his wife Harriet Stewart, daughter of the sixth earl of Galloway. She served Caroline of Brunswick, the ill
2 CONC -fated wife of George IV, as lady-in-waiting from the time of her marriage (1796), and remained with her until the princess left England for her European journeys (1813). When she returned to England as queen (1820), Lady Anne met
2 CONC  her at Montbord, and drove to London with her in the same carriage. Anne attended the queen during the thanksgiving service at St Paul's Cathedral to celebrate her acquittal in court (Nov 30, 1820), and later accompanied her body
2 CONC  to Brunswick for burial within the royal vault there. With the death of her kinsman, William Douglas, fourth duke of Queensberry (1810), she received a legacy of ten thousand punds, which she gave to her brother, Lord Archibald H
2 CONC amilton. Her position in later life was by no means affluent, and a volume of memoirs, purporting to be written by her, but published without her permission, appeared in London (1832) entitled The Secret History of the Court of En
2 CONC gland from the Accession of George III to the Death of George IV (1832).
