1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hutchins_Gordon_Burton
2 CONT
2 CONT Hutchins Gordon Burton (1774 or 1782 – 21 April 1836) was the 22nd Governor of the U.S. state of North Carolina from 1824 to 1827. Some sources indicate that he was not affiliated with any party at the time, although he was associ
2 CONC ated, according to other sources, with the Federalist Party and later with the National Republican Party.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Virginia, Burton was sent to Granville County, North Carolina to live with his uncle, Revolutionary War Colonel and politician Robert Burton, when his father died. Young Burton went on to practice law, serve in the North C
2 CONC arolina General Assembly, and be elected by that body as North Carolina Attorney General. He served as Attorney General from 1810 until his resignation in 1816.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burton moved to Halifax Town in 1817 and practiced law. He was elected to the state House of Commons on August 14, 1817 and served a single one-year term. On August 12, 1819, Burton was elected to the United States House of Repres
2 CONC entatives for the term 1819-1821. He was supported both by the Federalists, who were strong in Halifax Town, and also by the Democratic-Republican Party. Burton was re-elected in 1821 and 1823. He resigned from Congress on March 2
2 CONC 3, 1824.
2 CONT
2 CONT The North Carolina state legislature elected Burton the Governor of the state in 1824 and re-elected him in 1825 and 1826. During his term, President John Quincy Adams appointed him governor of Arkansas, but Burton was not confirm
2 CONC ed by the United States Senate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burton died in 1836 while visiting relatives in Iredell County, North Carolina and is buried at Unity Presbyterian Church in Lincoln County, North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ghost
2 CONT
2 CONT The circumstances of Governor Burton’s death have long been part of family oral tradition and North Carolina lore. As Armistead C. Gordon, Esq. recounts in Marguerite du Pont Lee’s Virginia Ghosts and Others, “Among the historic h
2 CONC omes in the old County and town of Halifax, N.C. is “Rocky Hill” the summer residence of Governor Hutchins G. Burton, a distinguished lawyer and statesman of the Revolutionary period, who married a daughter of Willie Jones, the fa
2 CONC mous Jeffersonian-Republican leader through whose efforts the adoption of the Federal Constitution was defeated in the Hillsboro Convention of North Carolina because it contained no Bill of Rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Burton lived at “The Grove,” the residence of his father-in-law, Willie Jones, in the town of Halifax where John Paul, the young Scotch sailor, spent some months prior to entering the American Navy at the beginning of the
2 CONC  Revolution, and in honor of whose owners, Mr. and Mrs. Jones; he adopted the name of John Paul Jones.
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Burton and his family were staying at Rocky Hill in the spring of 1836, when he was called to Texas where he owned a large tract of land. Setting out by stage coach, he arrived at Salisbury in the western part of the Stat
2 CONC e and stopped there on his way south to look after some business in court. Meeting his cousin Robert Burton of Lincoln County he went home with him to spend a few days. On the trip to Lincoln County he and his cousin visited the W
2 CONC ayside Inn and spent the night. Here he was taken ill and died within twenty-four hours, April 21, 1836.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife [Sarah “Sallie” Welsh Jones Burton] had been on a visit and was returning to Rocky Hill about dusk in her carriage driven by her servant William and accompanied by an infant grandchild and a nurse. As they approached the 
2 CONC house which is situated on a high hill she and William saw Governor Burton coming down the hill on a white horse which he usually rode. Her attention was distracted momentarily by the crying of the child and when she looked again 
2 CONC expecting her husband to speak as he approached the carriage both rider and horse had vanished.
2 CONT
2 CONT On account of the slow mail facilities of the time, Mrs. Burton did not hear of the Governor’s death until three weeks after it occurred, and she then learned that he had died at the very hour when the apparition had appeared to h
2 CONC er and her carriage driver.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rocky Hill which is situated near Ringwood in Halifax County was still standing in 1918 and was then owned by Mr. S. Harrison. The story of Governor Burton’s apparition is well authenticated and has persisted in Halifax County for
2 CONC  nearly a hundred years.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in Unity Churchyard, Lincoln County."
2 CONT
