1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Had eleven children.
2 CONT
2 CONT (According to http://www.victorianvilla.com/sims-mitchell/history/whittle_william.htm ):
2 CONT
2 CONT William Conway was the oldest of nine Whittle brothers (James was second). His daughter-in-law Ruth Drewry Whittle compiled the following account:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Conway Whittle … was in the United States Navy and remained in it until the Civil War, when he resigned and was made Commodore in the Confederate service.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was engaged in the naval part of the Mexican War in which he was wounded, and in the Confederate service rendered much service on York River and along the Mississippi and its tributaries and commanded the naval forces at New Or
2 CONC leans when it fell. It was at Vera Cruz that he was wounded in the Mexican War while charging a parapet at the head of his men that he had landed in open boats under heavy fire. He was also for two consecutive years on the Coast o
2 CONC f Africa as commander of the Sloop of War, Dale, suppressing the slave trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT After resigning his services in the U. S. Navy, he first tendered his services to Gov. John Letcher, the War Governor of Virginia, and was sent to Yorktown to look after the river defenses there. He then accepted the Commission of
2 CONC  Commodore from the Confederate States Government and remained in the service until the close of the war. He was in command at Jackson, Miss., New Orleans and elsewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war he lived for some years at his home, “The Anchorage,” at Buchanan, Virginia, but when his children grew up and moved away he lived about among them, and died at the residence of his son-in-law, Coles Terry, at Bent M
2 CONC ountain, Virginia. He was on the Coast of Africa in 1855 when the yellow fever plague visited Norfolk, in which his wife, Elizabeth Beverley Sinclair and his oldest son, Arthur Sinclair, lost their lives. His second wife [a Mrs. H
2 CONC amner] died very soon after he went to Buchanan to live.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among W. C. Whittle's eleven children were Lt. W. C. Whittle, Jr., CSN (executive officer of the C. S. S. Shenandoah); and Judge Stafford Gorman Whittle
2 CONT
2 CONT (According to http://history-sites.com/cgi-bin/bbs53x/cwnavy/webbbs_config.pl?noframes;read=3543 ):
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain William Conway Whittle was born in Norfolk, Va. in 1805 and was appointed a midshipman in the U.S. Navy May 10th, 1820. He served in various positions and in every grade in the "old navy" from midshipman to commander inclu
2 CONC sive, and on a large number of vessels, among which were the Ontario,Fairfield, Columbia, Brandywine and Ohio. He was in Florida during the Seminole disturbances, and in the Mexican War he was wounded at the Battle of Tuspan, and 
2 CONC afterwards commanded the dispatch steamer Col. Harvey. In 1853 he commanded the sloop Decatur on the banks of Newfoundland, and in 1854 and 1855 the U.S. sloop Dale on the coast of Africa. He resigned from the U.S. navy in 4/20/18
2 CONC 61 upon the secession of Virginia, and was in the Virginia navy. On June 11th, 1861, he was transffered to the C.S. navy. He commanded the naval defences on York River, Va., and the Confederate flotilla on the upper Mississipi, an
2 CONC d the naval station at New Orleans. He honorably served the Confederacy in various places and during the whole war. On October 23rd, 1862, he was promoted to Captain, to rank from February 8th, 1862 as well as the rank of Brigadie
2 CONC r General in the Confederate Army after the Battle of Mobile.His first wife, Elizabeth Sinclair Whittle died of Yellow Fever during an infestation brought into Norfolk during the summer of 1855 by the merchantman Franklin, as did 
2 CONC his eldest son, Arthur, while Whittle was asea as master of the U.S.S. Dale. He died in Virginia in 1878. William Conway Whittle died on Bent Mountain, Virginia. The family plot he was interred in was Magnolia Cemetery, Mount Plea
2 CONC sent, Charleston, South Carolina. It's as if a plague of stone-eating locusts decended on Magnolia Cemetery in 2005, whole plots of headstones disappeared from the place. Elizabeth Reed is no longer there. 11/11/09 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1860 Norfolk, VA Census lists the family of Captain USN William C. Whittle: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann E (2nd wife) 1821 (39) 
2 CONT
2 CONT William C., officer USN 1840 (20) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sallie Kennon 1842 (18) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane E. 1844 (16) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beverley Kennon 1846 (14) (Male) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth 1848 (12) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Hammand 1849 (11) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stafford G. 1850 (10) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conway M. 1852 (8) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilberta 1854 (6) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Questioning of Captain Whittle by the Confederate Senate regarding events preceding the fall of New Orleans, Series II, Volume I, Pages 445-446 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Conway Whittle, Jr., First Officer of the C.S.S. Shenandoah 1840-1920 He is buried in Cedar Grove Cemetery, Norfolk, as are the Sinclair. There is one other burial plot in Virginia near the Tenant House that contains Whitt
2 CONC les, as well as several in D.C. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Frome Rhea County, Tennessee Confederate Military records: 
2 CONT
2 CONT NAME: Whittle, Archimedes PENSION #: S15825 UNIT: 26th Inf. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The World War I Draft Registration Cards, 1917-1918 indicates that William Conway Whittle Jr. served in the military, and lived in Norfolk (Independent City), Virginia. This military record may include the soldier's home address, 
2 CONC occupation, dates served, the rank at induction and discharge, and state served. Some pension and draft registration records have other information about soldiers (like birth date and place) and their family members or surviving w
2 CONC idows. World War I Draft Registration Cards, 1917-1918 and other military records can trace the Whittle Jr.'s family service in the Revolutionary War, War of 1812, Civil War, Spanish American War, World War 1, and other national c
2 CONC onflicts. 
