1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (According to http://www.victorianvilla.com/sims-mitchell/history/whittle_james.htm ):
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his first wife, Mary, lived at the property now known as The Columns on 214 South Main Street in Chatham. After the birth of their daughter (also named Mary), his wife Mary died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Whittle later married his cousin Cornelia Skipwith of Mecklenburg County. They built and lived at Eldon, just east of Chatham. Cornelia died after the birth of their daughter Matoaka.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Whittle was active in a number of business enterprises, perhaps most notably the promotion and finance of a railroad from Lynchburg to Danville. (That railroad eventually became a portion of the main line of the Southern Rai
2 CONC lway, now the Norfolk and Southern.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875, as the railroad was being completed, Whittle built the Sims-Mitchell House for his daughter Matoaka and her husband William E. Sims, at a location across the tracks and just up the hill from the original Chatham station. 
2 CONC (The next rail stop north of Chatham, Whittles Station, is named for James Whittle.)
2 CONT
2 CONT At his death, Whittle owned significant portions of Chatham and adjacent land on the west side of town along the railroad, and over 5,000 acres of land in Pittsylvania County including his beloved Eldon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anecdotes from the period indicate that James Whittle was highly reputed for his honor, integrity, and Christian devotion. His marker in the Chatham Burial Park is inscribed, "I know that my Redeemer liveth."
