1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (According to http://www.victorianvilla.com/sims-mitchell/history/whittle_francis.htm ):
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis McNeece was the seventh of nine Whittle brothers (James was second). A relative compiled the following account:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Right Rev. Francis [McNeece Whittle], D. D., L. L. D., graduated from the Theological Seminary of Virginia in 1847; was ordained deacon in St. Paul's Church, Alexandria the same year and ordained Priest in St. Paul's, Charlest
2 CONC on, W. Va., Oct. 8th, 1848, by Bishop William Meade. He was successively Rector of Kanawha Parish, Kanawha County, West Va. 1847-48; St. James, Wortham Parish, Goochland County, Va. 1849-52; Grace, Berryville, Va. 1857-68. At the 
2 CONC Council of Virginia held 1867 he was elected Assistant Bishop and became Bishop April 5, 1876.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Emily Cary Fairfax, daughter of Wilson Cary Fairfax and Lucy A. Griffith#8230;on May 15, 1848. The daughters: Mary Anne, died at Petersburg, was buried at Blandford Nov. 18, 1862; Julia, died during the Civil War at Eld
2 CONC on Plantation near Chatham, Virginia, and was buried there; Jane Eliza, died [Jan. 1870?] at Hopkinsville, aged about 12 or 14 years, where she was going to school at her relatives, the Misses Murray.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note added 1 August 2022: Bishop Whittle was renowned for his "low church" approach to liturgy and churchmanship, resisting Anglo-Catholic movements within the Episcopal Church in the late 19th century. In a humorous aside about B
2 CONC ishop Whittle, the twelfth Bishop of Virginia (Coadjutor, 1984-1985; Diocesan, 1985-2009), Peter James Lee, shared Whittle's death bed last words with Bishop Andrew Waldo (Upper South Carolina VIII, and Whittle's grand nephew, thr
2 CONC ee times removed): "My only regret," Whittle is said to have uttered, "is that I was unable to rid the Church of the scourge of Anglo-Catholicism." His legacy and Anglican Catholicism continue thrive in the Episcopal Church. --And
2 CONC rew Waldo
