1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Boileau Wikipedia: John Boileau]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Peter Boileau, 1st Baronet FRS, DL, JP (Tuesday, 2 September 1794 – Tuesday, 9 March 1869) was a British baronet and archaeologist.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early life''
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Hertford Street in London's district Mayfair, he was the eldest son of John Peter Boileau and his wife Henrietta Pollen, the eldest daughter of John Pollen. His family claimed descendancy of Étienne Boileau, one of the fir
2 CONC st known provosts of Paris. He was educated at Eton College and went then to Merton College, Oxford. In 1813, Boileau joined the British Army and was commissioned as 2nd lieutenant into the Rifle Corps, which his uncle Coote Manni
2 CONC ngham had established. After four years service, he was put on halfpay in 1817. He bought an estate in Ketteringham in 1836, which he later expanded with a Gothic hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Career''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1838, Boileau was created a baronet, of Tacolnestone Hall, in the County of Norfolk. He was elected a fellow of the Royal Society in 1843 and was appointed High Sheriff of Norfolk in 1844. When one year later the Norfolk and No
2 CONC rwich Archaeological Society was founded, Boileau was nominated one of its vice-presidents until 1849, after which he became the Society's president. He joined the Society of Antiquaries of London in 1852 and by the recommendation
2 CONC  of Philip Stanhope, 5th Earl Stanhope, he was chosen a vice-president in 1858, a post he occupied for seven years with only a break in 1863. Boileau was additionally vice-president of the Zoological Society of London and of the R
2 CONC oyal Statistical Society. He served in the same capacity for the Institute of Archaeology and for the Royal Society of Arts. Boileau further held a fellowship in the Geological Society of London and was vice-president of the Royal
2 CONC  Institution as well as the British Science Association. He represented Norfolk both as a deputy lieutenant as well as a justice of the peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Personal life''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1825, he married Lady Catherine Sarah Elliot-Murray-Kynynmound, the third daughter of Gilbert Elliot-Murray-Kynynmound, 1st Earl of Minto and Anna Maria Amyand (a daughter of Sir George Amyand, 1st Baronet). They had four sons 
2 CONC and five daughters. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anna Maria Boileau (1826–1897), who married Rev. William Gurney, son of Daniel Gurney and Lady Harriett Hary (daughter of William Hay, 17th Earl of Erroll). They had issue.
2 CONT * John Elliot Boileau (1827–1861), who died unmarried.
2 CONT * Caroline Mary Boileau (circa 1829–1877), who died unmarried.
2 CONT * Sir Francis George Boileau, 2nd Baronet
2 CONT * Edmund William Pollen Boileau (1831–1883), who married Bridget Walsh and had issue.
2 CONT * Agnes Lucy Boileau (circa 1833–1881), who married Hon. William John Borlase Warren Venables-Vernon, son of George Venables-Vernon, 5th Baron Vernon.
2 CONT * Lieutenant Charles Augustus Penrhyn Boileau (1835–1855)
2 CONT * Mary Georgina Boileau (1836–1910), who died unmarried.
2 CONT * Theressa Anna Catherine Boileau (circa 1840–1872), who died unmarried.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife died in 1862 and Boileau survived her until 1869, having suffered on chronic bronchitis in his last years. Sir John died on Tuesday, 9 March 1869 at Torquay and was buried in the family's vault in Ketteringham. His oldest
2 CONC  son John having predeceased him in 1861, he was succeeded in the baronetcy by his second son Francis.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Descendants''
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his daughter Agnes, he was a grandfather of Reginald Venables-Vernon.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/104482610/john-peter-boileau findagrave: Sir John Peter Boileau (1794-1869)]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Name: Sir John Peter Boileau
2 CONT * Born: Tuesday, 2 September 1794
2 CONT * Died: Tuesday, 9 March 1869 (aged 74)
2 CONT * Buried: [St Peter's Churchyard, Church Road, Ketteringham, Norfolk NR18 9RS 52.57780, 1.19210], England
2 CONT * About: 1st Baronet FRS, DL, JP, British baronet and archaeologist. He he was the eldest son of John Peter Boileau and his wife Henrietta, the eldest daughter of John Pollen. His family claimed descendancy of Étienne Boileau, one
2 CONC  of the first known provosts of Paris. He was educated at Eton College and went then to Merton College, Oxford. In 1813, Boileau joined the British Army and was commissioned as 2nd lieutenant into the Rifle Corps, which his uncle 
2 CONC Coote Manningham had established. After four years service, he was put on halfpay in 1817. He bought an estate in Ketteringham in 1836, which he later expanded with a Gothic hall. In 1838, he was created a baronet, of Tacolnestone
2 CONC  Hall, in the County of Norfolk. He was elected a fellow of the Royal Society in 1843 and was appointed High Sheriff of Norfolk in 1844. When one year later the Norfolk and Norwich Archaeological Society was founded, Boileau was n
2 CONC ominated one of its vice-presidents until 1849, after which he became the Society's president. He served in the same capacity for the Institute of Archaeology and for the Royal Society of Arts. He further held a fellowship in the 
2 CONC Geological Society of London and was vice-president of the Royal Institution as well as the British Science Association. In 1825, he married Catherine Sarah, the third daughter of Gilbert Elliot-Murray-Kynynmound, 1st Earl of Mint
2 CONC o.
