1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=71621560
2 CONT
2 CONT Executive officer of the Confederate warship Shenandoah, which fired the last shot of the Civil War, in the Bering Sea, north of the Aleutian islands of Alaska.
2 CONT
2 CONT (According to http://history-sites.com/cgi-bin/bbs53x/cwnavy/webbbs_config.pl?noframes;read=3543 ):
2 CONT
2 CONT William Conway Whittle, Jr., First Officer of the C.S.S. Shenandoah 1840-1920 He is buried in Cedar Grove Cemetery, Norfolk, as are the Sinclair. There is one other burial plot in Virginia near the Tenant House that contains Whitt
2 CONC les, as well as several in D.C. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (From Rhea County, Tennessee Confederate Military records): 
2 CONT
2 CONT NAME: Whittle, Archimedes PENSION #: S15825 UNIT: 26th Inf. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The World War I Draft Registration Cards, 1917-1918 indicates that William Conway Whittle Jr. served in the military, and lived in Norfolk (Independent City), Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Register of Alumni, United States Academy Alumni Association, Vol. I, (1845-1915)] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Master, Lieutenant CSN William C. Whittle, born in Virginia, appointed from Virginia, resigned May 15, 1861, died January 5, 1920 in Norfolk, Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Graduated number 10 of 15 graduates, class of 1858. Of interest, George Dewey graduated number 5. 
