1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A J.P. in Birmingham. A quote from an article in 'Our Magistrates on the the bench' reads- "Mary Ryland was deservedly beloved by her family and friends. She inherited much of her ancestors gentleness and ability. 
2 CONC She was never known to express herself uncharitably in word or deed. She was a devoted loving mother and affectionate wife. She suffered a long lingering illness with great Christian fortitude, and passed away in her sleep, like h
2 CONC er own mother on 31st October 1872. Her memorial is to be found occupying the whole south transept window in the Church of St. Martin in Birmingham's Bullring. This window was commissioned by her husband Thomas Ryland in 1876 from
2 CONC  Edward (later Sir Edward) Burne-Jones, the Pre-Raphaelite artist. Burne-Jones had attended King Edwards School in New Street, where it is said his contemporaries, including George Crowther Ryland, eldest brother of Howard. Burne-
2 CONC Jones had gone into partnership with William Morris and it was in the studios of Morris and Co. that the window was produced, their first in Birmingham. It is a large window comprising three tiers, the lowest depicting five biblic
2 CONC al scenes and above these there are two rows with ten full-length figures- Christ, Soloman, Moses, David, saints, kings and prophets. The tracery in the Gothic arch above fourteen panels of angels. Some of the scenes had been desi
2 CONC gned previously and used elsewhere, including Calcutta Cathedral, but three were new at a cost of £10 each. The backgrounds and borders remind one strongly of the style of wallpaper and furnishing fabrics that still bear the Willi
2 CONC am Morris name but the window as a whole is a striking and beautiful example of Burne-Jones work and, having survived the depredations of the Luftwaffe, is the pride of St.Martins.
