0 @N6000000002188436701@ NOTE On December 14, 1840 Pope Gregory XVI named Ludovico a Cardinal of the Roman Catholic Church.  However, this action was done "in pectore", which meant it was not public knowledge.  The Cardinal served as the titular Archbishop of 
1 CONC Efesco, and was a papal nuncio in Austria.  During the Papal Consistory of April 21, 1845 the Cardinals selection by the Pope was made public.  The Cardinal participated in the Papal Conclave of June 14 through June 16, 1846 which
1 CONC  elected Giovanni Maria Mastai Ferretti, Archbishop-Bishop of Imola as Pope Pius IX.
0 @N6000000002188429648@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      Duke Charles Ferdinand of Berry, youngest son of Charles, first Count of Artois and later King Charles X of France. The Duke served in the Prince of Bourbon-Condé's army against the French Revolution.
1 CONT The Duke was stabbed to death by a lone fanatic as he left the Opera on February 13, 1820.  His assassination during the reign of King Louis XVIII was an attempt to extinguish the Bourbon line, and gave the ultraroyalists the oppo
1 CONC rtunity to turn King Louis XVIII against the liberals. Berry's posthumous son was Henri, the Count of Chambord.
0 @N6000000002176689205@ NOTE He was the adopted son of Napoleon I and a French army officer and aide to
1 CONT Napoleon I. He received the title of prince & was appointed archchancellor of
1 CONT state by Napoleon who was genuinely interested in the walefare of his two
1 CONT stepchildren, later both adopted. In 1805, when Napoleon proclaimed himself
1 CONT king of Italy, Eugene became viceroy there, where Eugene built roads and
1 CONT introduced the French legal system. In 1806, he married the daughter of
1 CONT Maximilian I and received the title of Duke von Leuchenberg.
